-- Trade lifecycle - taking a commodity trade from booking to
-- settlement, with the rules about who may confirm a price, which price
-- may be settled on, and how much may be settled enforced by the runtime
-- instead of by a status column and a prayer.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain. A trader strikes a deal - buy or sell a
-- quantity of some commodity (power, gas, oil) for delivery over a
-- future period - and *captures* it: the terms, plus the price they
-- believe they struck. That captured price is only the trader's word
-- for it. Before anyone settles money against the trade, a separate
-- desk - the middle office - independently *confirms* it against the
-- counterparty's own record, and the price they confirm becomes the
-- official one. The trade then *settles*, often in slices over the
-- delivery period. Terms can also be *amended* later - a quantity
-- re-agreed, a mis-keyed figure corrected - sometimes effective from a
-- date in the past.
--
-- Three ideas do the real work, and this example is built around them.
--
-- First, a trade is never in a single "status". It is captured, then
-- also confirmed, then also settled - each step admits another claim,
-- and the earlier claims stay on the record. There is no
-- `status = "confirmed"` field to overwrite: a trade is "confirmed"
-- precisely because a confirmation claim stands for it. The phase is
-- the accumulation of admitted claims, and the history reads itself.
--
-- Second, the same trade carries two prices at two standings. The
-- trader's captured price is an estimate - real, recorded, but not
-- something anyone may settle on. The middle office's confirmed price
-- is the official one, marked by a pointer to the figure currently in
-- force, and settlement will rely on that. If the middle office later
-- finds it set the wrong official price, it corrects it the honest way:
-- the old figure stays on the record, a corrected figure is admitted
-- alongside it, and the in-force pointer moves to the new one.
--
-- Third, a trade's *terms* live on two separate time axes, and keeping
-- them apart is what lets the books answer questions honestly years
-- later. One axis is when the system *recorded* a claim - that is the
-- audit log, and any past moment can be replayed "as of" a transition.
-- The other axis is when a claim is *effective in the world* - the date
-- a quantity takes force. We do not store effective time in special
-- database columns; it is just a date carried on an ordinary claim. So a
-- trade's quantity is a *versioned* term, each version carrying the date
-- it becomes effective. An amendment can be backdated: recorded today,
-- effective from an earlier date. The two axes together answer both
-- "what was effective on that date?" and "what did we know when?" - and
-- the answer to the first can change as the second moves, because new
-- knowledge (a backdated amendment) arrives.
--
-- Here is how Morpholog thinks about this. It does not store "facts" -
-- it stores *claims* it has agreed to admit because they obey the
-- rules. Two kinds of rule do the work:
--
--   * an `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all admitted state,
--     forever. The runtime refuses to commit any change that would
--     break one - a trade can never be settled, in total, for more than
--     the quantity effective on the date being settled.
--   * a `require` is a gate on a single action, checked the moment you
--     act and never again. Whether the actor confirming or amending
--     holds authority for that commodity is settled when they act; a
--     decision made today stays valid even if that authority is later
--     withdrawn. An invariant governs what is true; a gate governs what
--     you may do.

program trade_lifecycle

-- The vocabulary. `TradeCaptured` is the trade's immutable identity -
-- which commodity, which direction; its amendable *quantity* lives
-- separately. `TradeTerms` is a versioned terms record carrying the
-- effective date its quantity takes force from; capture admits the first
-- version, an amendment admits a later one. `TradeTermsSupersedes`
-- records which terms version amended which (lineage for audit).
-- `CapturedPrice` is the trader's estimate, recorded but carrying no
-- settlement standing. `MayConfirm` is a desk's authority to confirm or
-- amend for a commodity. `TradeConfirmed` records the confirmation event
-- against the counterparty, stamping in the actor who confirmed it.
-- `OfficialPrice` is an official price figure; `CurrentOfficialPrice` is
-- the retractable pointer naming which figure is in force now;
-- `OfficialPriceSupersedes` records price-correction lineage.
-- `TradeSettled` records one settlement slice - its quantity, the
-- official price id it relied on, and the business date it is effective
-- on (not the date it was recorded).
predicate TradeCaptured(trade: Subject, commodity: Subject, direction: Subject)
predicate TradeTerms(trade: Subject, version_id: Subject, quantity: Decimal, delivery_period: Subject, effective_from: Date)
predicate TradeTermsSupersedes(new_version_id: Subject, prior_version_id: Subject)
predicate CapturedPrice(trade: Subject, price: Decimal)
predicate MayConfirm(principal: Subject, commodity: Subject)
predicate TradeConfirmed(trade: Subject, counterparty: Subject, confirmation_id: Subject, confirmed_by: Subject)
predicate OfficialPrice(trade: Subject, price: Decimal, official_price_id: Subject)
predicate CurrentOfficialPrice(trade: Subject, official_price_id: Subject)
predicate OfficialPriceSupersedes(new_official_price_id: Subject, prior_official_price_id: Subject)
predicate TradeSettled(trade: Subject, settled_qty: Decimal, settlement_id: Subject, official_price_id: Subject, effective_on: Date)
-- The read-side projection (defined by the `derived` block at the foot of
-- the file): one row per terms version, keyed by its identity and
-- effective date, carrying the quantity in force from that date.
predicate TermsTimeline(trade: Subject, version_id: Subject, delivery_period: Subject, effective_from: Date, quantity: Decimal)

-- Intents are messages the system sends to the outside world after a
-- change commits. The settlement-request intent is the one that asks a
-- downstream system to actually move the money and the commodity:
-- Morpholog records that the settlement is legitimate and leaves the
-- execution of it outside.
intent TradeCapturedAdmitted(trade: Subject)
intent TradeTermsAmended(trade: Subject, new_version_id: Subject, prior_version_id: Subject)
intent ConfirmAuthorityGranted(principal: Subject, commodity: Subject)
intent TradeConfirmedAdmitted(trade: Subject, confirmation_id: Subject, official_price_id: Subject)
intent OfficialPriceCorrected(trade: Subject, new_official_price_id: Subject, prior_official_price_id: Subject)
intent TradeSettlementRequested(settlement_id: Subject, trade: Subject, settled_qty: Decimal)


-- The rules that must always hold. Any change that would leave the
-- world violating one of these is rejected outright. Read `implies` as
-- "whenever the left is true, the right must be too".

-- The official price in force can never point at nothing: every current
-- pointer must name an official price figure that actually exists.
invariant current_official_price_has_a_figure:
    CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, official_price_id) implies OfficialPrice(trade, _, official_price_id)

-- There is exactly one official price in force at a time for a trade.
invariant at_most_one_current_official_price:
    CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, a) and CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, b) implies a = b

-- An official price id identifies one figure: two OfficialPrice claims
-- sharing the id must agree on the trade and the price.
invariant official_price_id_identifies_one_figure:
    OfficialPrice(trade_a, price_a, opid) and OfficialPrice(trade_b, price_b, opid) implies (trade_a = trade_b and price_a = price_b)

-- An official price always belongs to a trade that was actually
-- captured.
invariant official_price_has_captured_trade:
    OfficialPrice(trade, _, _) implies TradeCaptured(trade, _, _)

-- The price-correction history stays a clean chain, never a fork: an
-- official price figure can be superseded by at most one direct
-- successor.
invariant official_price_chain_no_fork:
    OfficialPriceSupersedes(new_a, old) and OfficialPriceSupersedes(new_b, old) implies new_a = new_b

-- A trade id identifies one captured trade: two TradeCaptured claims
-- sharing the id must agree on commodity and direction. This pins the
-- structural uniqueness behind the trade's identity.
invariant at_most_one_capture_per_trade:
    TradeCaptured(trade, commodity_a, direction_a) and TradeCaptured(trade, commodity_b, direction_b) implies (commodity_a = commodity_b and direction_a = direction_b)

-- Every terms version belongs to a trade that was actually captured -
-- the same provenance discipline the official price obeys.
invariant terms_belong_to_captured_trade:
    TradeTerms(trade, _, _, _, _) implies TradeCaptured(trade, _, _)

-- A terms version id identifies one terms record: two TradeTerms claims
-- sharing the version id must agree on every field. Version ids carry
-- the amendment lineage, so they must be unambiguous.
invariant terms_version_id_identifies_one_record:
    TradeTerms(trade_a, v, qty_a, period_a, ef_a) and TradeTerms(trade_b, v, qty_b, period_b, ef_b) implies (trade_a = trade_b and qty_a = qty_b and period_a = period_b and ef_a = ef_b)

-- At most one terms version per effective date for a trade. Two versions
-- tied on the same effective date would make "the quantity effective on
-- that date" ambiguous, and the latest-effective lookup below relies on
-- it being a single number - so an amendment must take a distinct
-- effective date. (A same-date *correction* would need a supersession
-- tiebreak, the way the official price does; that is a separate move.)
invariant one_terms_version_per_effective_date:
    TradeTerms(trade, v_a, _, _, ef) and TradeTerms(trade, v_b, _, _, ef) implies v_a = v_b

-- The terms-amendment history stays a clean chain, never a fork: a terms
-- version can be superseded by at most one direct successor.
invariant trade_terms_chain_no_fork:
    TradeTermsSupersedes(new_a, old) and TradeTermsSupersedes(new_b, old) implies new_a = new_b

-- The quantitative rule at the heart of settlement, now on the effective
-- axis. A trade settles in slices, each effective on a business date. For
-- each settlement effective date `d`, the total quantity settled
-- effective on or before `d` must not exceed the quantity the terms in
-- force on `d` allow. "The terms in force on `d`" is the version with the
-- latest effective date that is on or before `d` - selected by saying no
-- other version effective by `d` has a later effective date. Backdating
-- an amendment to a higher quantity (effective on or before `d`) lifts
-- the cap governing the slices effective on `d`, so a slice that was over
-- the old quantity but within the new one becomes admissible. The
-- comparison is inclusive.
invariant settled_within_effective_terms:
    (TradeSettled(trade, _, _, _, d)
     and TradeTerms(trade, _, qty, _, ef) and ef on_or_before d
     and not (exists later_ef: TradeTerms(trade, _, _, _, later_ef) and later_ef on_or_before d and later_ef after ef))
    implies sum(s | TradeSettled(trade, s, _, _, sd) and sd on_or_before d) <= qty

-- A settlement id names one settlement slice: two TradeSettled claims
-- sharing the id must agree on every field. The settle gate already
-- refuses to reuse an id by the ordinary path; this is the backstop for
-- any other path, keeping the cumulative sum above honest.
invariant settlement_id_identifies_one_settlement:
    TradeSettled(trade_a, qty_a, sid, opid_a, eff_a) and TradeSettled(trade_b, qty_b, sid, opid_b, eff_b) implies (trade_a = trade_b and qty_a = qty_b and opid_a = opid_b and eff_a = eff_b)

-- Settlement can never run ahead of confirmation: a settled trade must
-- have been confirmed. The settle action relies on the official price in
-- force, which only a confirmed trade has, so this invariant guarantees
-- the property for all admitted state, by any path.
invariant settled_trade_was_confirmed:
    TradeSettled(trade, _, _, _, _) implies TradeConfirmed(trade, _, _, _)


-- The only ways state may change. A transformation proposes a set of
-- changes; the runtime applies them only if every invariant still holds
-- once they are in place. `admit` adds a claim; `retract` removes one;
-- `emit` queues a message to the outside world for after the commit.

-- A trader captures a new trade: its identity, the price they believe
-- they struck, and the first version of its terms - quantity and
-- delivery period, effective from the trade date. The gate refuses a
-- second capture under the same trade id, so a trade's identity is fixed
-- once booked; a change of quantity is an amendment, never a silent
-- overwrite. The captured price carries no settlement standing on its
-- own; only a confirmed price will.
transformation capture_trade(trade, commodity, direction, version_id, quantity, delivery_period, captured_on, price):
    require not TradeCaptured(trade, _, _)
    admit TradeCaptured(trade, commodity, direction)
    admit TradeTerms(trade, version_id, quantity, delivery_period, captured_on)
    admit CapturedPrice(trade, price)
    emit TradeCapturedAdmitted(trade)

-- Amend a trade's terms - re-agree a quantity, correct a delivery
-- period - effective from a date that may be in the past. The new
-- version is admitted alongside the old (the old stays on the record), a
-- lineage link records which version it amends, and the new version
-- takes a distinct effective date. The gates require confirmation
-- authority for the trade's commodity, that the version being amended
-- exists, and that it has not already been amended, so the chain never
-- forks. A settlement already made under the prior terms stays standing;
-- the amendment governs only what the effective-quantity cap allows next.
transformation amend_trade_terms(trade, prior_version_id, new_version_id, quantity, delivery_period, effective_from):
    require TradeCaptured(trade, commodity, _) and MayConfirm(actor, commodity)
    require TradeTerms(trade, prior_version_id, _, _, _)
    require not TradeTermsSupersedes(_, prior_version_id)
    admit TradeTerms(trade, new_version_id, quantity, delivery_period, effective_from)
    admit TradeTermsSupersedes(new_version_id, prior_version_id)
    emit TradeTermsAmended(trade, new_version_id, prior_version_id)

-- Grant a desk the authority to confirm or amend for a commodity.
-- Confirmation is a middle-office responsibility, scoped per commodity
-- and kept separate from whoever captured the trade.
transformation grant_confirm_authority(principal, commodity):
    admit MayConfirm(principal, commodity)
    emit ConfirmAuthorityGranted(principal, commodity)

-- The middle office confirms the trade against the counterparty and sets
-- the official price. The first gate ties the authority to the trade's
-- own commodity - not just any commodity the actor happens to hold. The
-- rest insist the trade is not already confirmed and has no official
-- price in force yet.
transformation confirm_trade(trade, counterparty, confirmation_id, official_price_id, confirmed_price):
    require TradeCaptured(trade, commodity, _) and MayConfirm(actor, commodity)
    require not TradeConfirmed(trade, _, _, _)
    require not CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, _)
    admit TradeConfirmed(trade, counterparty, confirmation_id, actor)
    admit OfficialPrice(trade, confirmed_price, official_price_id)
    admit CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, official_price_id)
    emit TradeConfirmedAdmitted(trade, confirmation_id, official_price_id)

-- Correct an official price the honest way. The old figure stays on the
-- record; a corrected figure is admitted alongside it; the in-force
-- pointer moves to the new figure; a lineage link records that the new
-- one supersedes the old. The gates require confirmation authority for
-- the trade's commodity, that the prior figure is the one currently in
-- force, and that it has not already been superseded, so the correction
-- chain never forks. A settlement already made under the prior official
-- price is left untouched - it stays a true record of what was settled
-- that day; the correction governs only what may be settled next.
transformation correct_official_price(trade, prior_official_price_id, new_official_price_id, corrected_price):
    require TradeCaptured(trade, commodity, _) and MayConfirm(actor, commodity)
    require CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, prior_official_price_id)
    require not OfficialPriceSupersedes(_, prior_official_price_id)
    admit OfficialPrice(trade, corrected_price, new_official_price_id)
    admit OfficialPriceSupersedes(new_official_price_id, prior_official_price_id)
    retract CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, prior_official_price_id)
    admit CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, new_official_price_id)
    emit OfficialPriceCorrected(trade, new_official_price_id, prior_official_price_id)

-- Settle the trade, in whole or in a slice effective on a business date.
-- Two gates, both operational preconditions: there must be an official
-- price in force to settle on (the gate binds which one), and the
-- settlement id must be unused. That second gate makes the settlement id
-- an *idempotency key* - replaying a settlement is refused before it can
-- request a second downstream payment. A trade may settle many times,
-- each slice under its own fresh id, recording the official price id it
-- relied on and the business date it is effective on, so "what price did
-- we settle at?" keeps a truthful answer per slice even after a later
-- correction moves the pointer. The structural truths are left to the
-- invariants: that a settled trade was confirmed, and that the total
-- settled effective on or before any date never exceeds the quantity the
-- terms in force on that date allow. How much money moves is computed
-- downstream; Morpholog governs only that the settlement is legitimate.
transformation settle_trade(trade, settled_qty, settlement_id, official_price_id, effective_on):
    require CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, official_price_id)
    require not TradeSettled(_, _, settlement_id, _, _)
    admit TradeSettled(trade, settled_qty, settlement_id, official_price_id, effective_on)
    emit TradeSettlementRequested(settlement_id, trade, settled_qty)


-- A read-side view: the trade's terms as a timeline, one row per version
-- carrying the date its quantity becomes effective. Replayed "as of" a
-- past transition (the recorded-time axis), this answers "what quantity
-- was effective on a given date, as we knew it then?" - and the answer to
-- a fixed effective date can differ across two replays when a backdated
-- amendment was recorded between them. That is bitemporality with no
-- valid-time columns: just a date on a claim and the audit log.
derived TermsTimeline(trade, version_id, delivery_period, effective_from):
    over TradeTerms(trade, version_id, _, delivery_period, effective_from)
    value quantity = value TradeTerms(trade, version_id, _, delivery_period, effective_from)
